Description: <!-- What happened? What did you expect? -->

Cloak's JavaScript humanization layer patches `Frame` action methods by delegating them to the owning `Page` action methods. This appears to break iframe-scoped interactions.

Expected behavior:

`frame.click(selector)`, `frame.fill(selector, value)`, and Locator actions created from a frame should preserve the original Playwright frame scope. A selector inside an iframe should be resolved inside that iframe.

Actual behavior:

In `patchSingleFrame`, frame methods delegate to page methods:

```ts
(frame as any).click = async (selector, options) => {
  await (page as any).click(selector, options);
};

(frame as any).fill = async (selector, value, options) => {
  await (page as any).fill(selector, value, options);
};

(frame as any).type = async (selector, text, options) => {
  await (page as any).type(selector, text, options);
};
```

In Playwright, `page.click(selector)` delegates to the main frame only, while `frame.click(selector)` is scoped to that frame. So after Cloak patches a child frame, iframe-contained selectors may be searched in the main frame instead of the iframe.

This also affects frame-owned Locators, because Playwright Locator methods call their owning frame:

```js
locator.click() -> this._frame.click(this._selector, ...)
locator.fill()  -> this._frame.fill(this._selector, value, ...)
```

After Cloak patches `frame.click`, that frame-owned Locator effectively becomes:

```js
iframeLocator.click() -> frame.click(selector) -> page.click(selector)
```

That loses iframe scope.

