Additional notes:

Why this matters:

Many real-world forms and challenge flows are iframe-heavy. For those pages, preserving frame scope is not optional. Playwright intentionally separates:

```js
page.click(selector);   // main frame
frame.click(selector);  // specific frame
```

Cloak should preserve that contract while adding humanization.

This also matters for stealth and maintainability. If users discover that iframe `Locator` actions break under Cloak, they may work around it by adding coordinate bridges or custom monkey patches. Those workarounds can introduce new fingerprints, such as deterministic click positions or inconsistent action routing.

A better fix would be for Cloak's frame patches to preserve frame selector scope while still using Cloak-owned mouse/keyboard behavior. For example, frame actions could resolve/wait/actionability-check inside the frame, then execute the final mouse/keyboard action through the Cloak humanized page driver using the element's viewport coordinates.

